{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When baby boomers started working, about five of \nthem had to work to provide for every pensioner.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, this number has gone down to around three \nworkers per pensioner in rich countries. And this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will only get worse. Based on OECD projections, \n30 years from now, there will be less than two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17680,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "workers per single pensioner when the baby \nboomers all retire. This does not look good.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, of course, this raises the question. Can Gen \nZ and millennials actually afford to pay for all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boomer pensions? In France, this question is not a \ntheoretical math problem. It is already leading to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a neverending political crisis. In the capital, \nthousands of people taking to the streets after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "French President Emanuel Macron forced through \nan unpopular bill that raises the retirement age  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48240,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from 62 to 64 years old. And given that France is \nactually a relatively young rich world country and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56080,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that on top of pensions, medical costs will of \ncourse also skyrocket as the boomers turn in 75,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economists like professor Charles Goodheart from \nthe law school of economics have said that unless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI gives us a miracle far bigger than anything \nwe're seeing so far economically speaking the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75280,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next 40 years are not going to be anything like as \nwonderful as the last 40. So what can we expect?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Political crisis in France, the UK, and Germany \nmake it clear that the current pension system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where Gen Z and millennials pay boomers can only \nbe sustainable if we drastically increase the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92560,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retirement age, cut pensions, or if young people \naccept sky high taxes. But there's hope. Small  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100960,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "North European countries like Denmark, the \nNetherlands, and Iceland have systems where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every pensioner saves for their own retirement. \nAll of these savings are then pumped into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111360,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stock markets which have historically only gone \nup. Right? Right. Hm, it makes you wonder, have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these countries really found a magical pension \nsolution or are they blowing yet another financial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble? To answer these questions, our team has \nspent weeks interviewing experts, diving into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "various pension systems and population data of all \nrich countries around the world. And what we found  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137360,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was quite interesting. Denmark and the Netherlands \nmay be right, just not in the way they think. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before we get into how we can save boomer pensions \nand young people's incomes at the same time, we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first need to confront reality. What happens if we \ndo nothing? Here's chapter one, the unsustainable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality of our pension systems. Broadly speaking, \nthere are two ways countries finance their pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems. There are funded systems where workers \nsave for their own pension and the so-called pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as you go system where current workers pay for \ncurrent retirees. In practice, most countries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "use a mix of both systems, but the balance \nvaries widely. Countries like Germany, Spain,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Italy rely almost entirely on pay as you go \npensions where benefits are financed by today's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "workforce. By contrast, countries like Denmark and \nthe Netherlands depend much more heavily on funded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems where people save for their own retirement \nusing public or private pension funds that invest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200560,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in bond and stock markets. They use these returns \nearned there to minimize contributions. Right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countries like Italy, Germany, and France that \nrely on pay as you go systems are facing severe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pension crisis because it is very clear that \ntheir pension system was not built for a rapidly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aging population. Fewer workers that pay the same \ncontribution to support more retirees that want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a stable pension income. The math simply does \nnot work. Therefore, countries have tried four  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "controversial ways to make the system sustainable. \nThe first option is to raise the retirement age.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Germany, the UK, and the Netherlands are all \nmoving towards 67. Meanwhile, Italy will go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even further. They have tied the retirement age \nto rising life expectancy, meaning that it will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise to 70. However, as you can imagine, many \npeople who are close to retirement are not exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thrilled about this. Why should they pay the price \nfor the unsustainable pension system? This applies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially to workers in physically demanding \njobs. Therefore, Italy's Prime Minister Maloney  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265760,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is now facing strong pressure from labor unions \nto freeze the retirement age at 67. Luckily for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maloney, there are three more options. The second \noption is to reduce pension payments. So far,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this has only happened in case of massive \npolitical crisis such as when Greek pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payments were cut during the Greek debt crisis. \nWhat some governments have done though is pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smart. They pledged to cut pension payments in \nthe future, not today. After all, people are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less likely to protest getting a lower income if \nthey don't feel it yet. For example, in Germany,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302080,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the average pension was set to fall from 48% \nof the average wage to 45% by 2040. Still, here  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are a threepoint drop seemed actually a bit too \nfar for older workers especially, and they have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revolted causing politicians to raise this back \nto 46%. This then brings us to the third option,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "raising worker pension payments. For example, \nbetween 2018 and 2023, Canada has been gradually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing the contribution rate from 5 to 6%. And \nsimilarly, Mexico and South Korea have announced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worker contributions will have to increase. But \nagain, this is politically super tricky. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it comes to pension reforms, there are just no \ngood options. It seems. more retirees and fewer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "workers. Of course, someone will have to take a \nhit. This is why pay as you go pensions reforms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350320,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost always trigger massive public backlash. \nYou still have 2/3 of the French opposed to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pension reform. Although there is of course a \nsimpler option, for example, the most rapidly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aging country in the world has so far avoided \npolitically very hard pension choices by simply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing government borrowing. And honestly, \nwhile people like to think about government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inefficiencies and bureaucracies that are behind \never rising government debt, the cold hard reality  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that for most of the rich world, it has mostly \nbeen about increasing social security payments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to pensioners and ever increasing healthcare \ncosts that also mostly go to pensioners. Sadly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today it seems like more and more governments \nare hitting the limits of what they can borrow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without facing massive consequences. Both the UK \nand France recently flirted with a government debt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crisis. And not coincidentally, these are the two \ncountries with some of the most generous pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "payments. So, it seems like governments now have \nto make a really hard choice between three bad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "options. Number one, increase the pension age. \nThis will piss off Gen X and young boomers who  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are close to the pension age. Option number two, \ncut pension benefits. Again, this will piss off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428720,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boomer pensioners or again Gen Xers who are close \nto the pension age. So, there's also option three,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436640,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing taxes massively, ticking off Gen \nZ and millennial workers. Or do they? Could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it be that countries like Denmark and Netherlands \nhave discovered a magical fifth option? An option  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that seems to make the system both more fair and \nmore sustainable. What if workers saved for their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own retirement instead of relying on future \ngenerations to pay the bill? This is exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the promise of fully funded systems. Heavily \nfunded pension systems like those in Denmark,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Netherlands, and Iceland, by the \nway, are consistently ranked as being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most sustainable on the planet. And on the \nsurface, it's easy to see why. In these systems,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than paying for an older, potentially much \nlarger generation, Danish and Dutch workers mostly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "save for their own pensions via either private \nor collective pension funds. These funds then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invest savings in financial markets. Rather \nthan being funded by current workers, these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pension funds pay out a pension based on what \nthe workers saved, plus the returns the pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funds made on these investments in financial \nmarkets. This clearly looks more sustainable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a pay as you go system. Instead of relying \non a shrinking workforce to pay current pensions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retirement is financed by what each generation \nsaved plus financial market returns. And while  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial markets may go down from time to time, \nin the long run, financial markets can only go up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right? So, it seems like in these countries, \nGen Z, millennials, Gen X, and boomers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they just pay for their own pensions. Gen \nZ is not supporting a much larger boomer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generation. But is that actually true? Because \nthis story got me really suspicious. You see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as macroeconomist, I was trained to distinguish \nbetween what happens in the financial system and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what happens in the real economy. The economy of \ngoods and services, the total amount of output,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552800,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doctors, food, and care. Financial markets often \nact very differently than the underlying economy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet in the long run, financial assets like stocks \nin companies are merely claims on future goods  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and services. So if the economy suffers because \nof aging, then of course these companies will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570640,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start to suffer as well. In theory, stock market \nprices should reflect company prospects. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the short run, meaning decades, pretty long, \nthis does not have to be the case. You see what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also matters for financial markets is how much \nmoney people invest in them compared to spending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on other things. In fully funded systems, the \ngovernment has essentially forced people to invest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more in financial markets. So what happens when \na large generation like the baby boomers enters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the workforce? Millions of people start saving at \nthe same time. Pension funds are suddenly flooded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with cash and rush to buy financial assets. \nAs a consequence, prices of assets go up and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612160,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up and forever up. That is until the demographic \nsituation flips. If the boomers start retiring,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then all of the sudden people are taking out \nmore money from their pension funds than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller new younger generations are putting \nin. So if forced saving raised all asset prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629200,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the same time, then of course mass retirement \nshould in theory cause a sustained asset meltdown.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This simple logic is behind the asset meltdown \nhypothesis which state that once the boomer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generation retires, this flow will reverse \ncrashing assets. Pension funds now need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sell more and more assets to pay out pensions. \nBut this time the new generation who is supposed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to buy all of these assets is smaller. Already in \n1994, based on US demographics, Sylvester Sheber  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Professor John Schovin predicted that private \npension funds in the US would start emptying out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 2024. That's right. This problem will \nnot just hit countries with fully funded systems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like Denmark and the Netherlands. It will affect \nprivate pension funds all around the world, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676400,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boomers and Gen Xers have used to supplement their \npay as you go pensions. In the past 40 years, many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boomers have already saved for their own pensions. \nThis created massive demand for stocks and bonds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contributing to the massively impressive stock \nmarket gains that we have seen since the 1980s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "despite Western economies growing far more slowly \nthan in the years before. When they start selling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off assets, optimistic pension sustainability \ncalculations in countries like Denmark and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Netherlands could become meaningless. For example, \nthe Dutch have saved very, very conservatively.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So conservatively that a 2% average profit on \nasset markets would be enough to cover most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pension payments for the foreseeable future. But \nwhat if the asset meltdown hypothesis is correct?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if so many people retiring in the coming \ndecades means that financial markets will stop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729440,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rising forever and rather start falling for a very \nvery long time? In that case, countries with fully  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736880,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funded systems will again face the same difficult \nchoice that pay as you go countries like the UK,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Germany, and France face today. But is that \nindeed what will happen? Are fully funded systems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a financial trick that will not solve the \nfundamental demographic problem of fewer workers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per retiree? The answer is yes. And it won't \nwork. It doesn't matter whether you try and fund  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761680,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it because the cost of funding it will become \nincreasingly severe. Now I have to say luckily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economists have found that so far many pensioners \nactually do not perfectly spend down their pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773040,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "savings. Typically they die with substantial \nsaved up assets still in their name which are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then passed on to the next generation. So this \ncould soften the blow but still the underlying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demographics are hard to escape. Whether you \nare a pay as you go or a fully funded country,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cold hard demographic reality is that if \nmore people retire compared to those who work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less stuff is made while demand by pensioners \nremains high. Therefore, economists like Goodheart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802240,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and his co-author Pradhan predict that what they \ncall the great demographic reversal will lead to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809520,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decades of number one lower asset prices. Number \ntwo, higher inflation because all these pensioners  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do keep spending. Number three, higher taxes, \nand number four surprisingly lower inequality  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which perhaps the only positive takeaway. \nAfter all, if there are not enough workers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially in the low paid care sector, then \nthese workers can demand higher wages. Yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even these workers will face higher inflation. \nSo, overall, the reality will remain. Aging will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838560,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead to three tough political choices. Number one, \nretire later, number two, lower pension payments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or number three, increase taxes. Or will it? \nBecause actually when doing the research for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this video, a strange thought popped into my \nhead. Could the fully funded system be superior  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after all? At least for small aging countries \nlike Denmark and the Netherlands. You see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pay as you go system is national. German Gen Z \nworkers pay for German boomer pensioners. German  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demographics determine the sustainability of the \nGerman pension system right now. But Dutch and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876560,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Danish pension funds, they have invested the \nmajority of Danish and Dutch boomer pension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880880,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "savings abroad, especially in the US, which has \nquite a bit better demographic profile. So could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it be that while fully funded pension systems are \nessentially a trick that may cause a big asset  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894960,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meltdown when most boomers retire, smaller older \ncountries may avoid this scenario for a long time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901600,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if they invest in sufficiently young economies. We \nwill have to see. In fact, that is the conclusion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910000,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for this entire video. We will have to see. You \nsee, the world has simply never gone through this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive demographic transition before. So \nthe asset meltdown hypothesis and Goodheart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictions of increased inflation, higher \ntaxes, and lower inequality, these have never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been scientifically tested, at least not in the \nWest. Because interestingly, the asset meltdown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933360,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothesis may have accidentally been tested in \nChile during COVID-19. You see, to alleviate the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial stress many households felt during the \npandemic, the government allowed them to access  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945520,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their pension savings early. Guess what happened? \nNumber one, the local stock market crashed. Number  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953440,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two, there was a massive inflation spike. Number \nthree, and this is new, the government stepped in,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "essentially nationalizing the pension system, \nmaking it from relatively fully funded to pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as you go. That's right. Two of Goodart's main \npredictions immediately came true when Chile's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expension systems were drawn down. So sadly, I \nsuspect there is a real chance Professor Goodhart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978240,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will soon be proven right that the next 40 years \nare not going to be anything like as wonderful as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the last 40. That is unless we do actually see \na miracle from AI where honestly there are some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty incredible developments. But if you want \nto know more about those, I have to refer you to  "
    } ]
  }, {
